1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,533
One thousand orders, typed by five different people.

2
00:00:03,533 --> 00:00:11,238
Extra spaces, random capitals, the same order pasted twice, dates written three different ways, and prices stored as text.

3
00:00:11,238 --> 00:00:11,617
Watch.

4
00:00:11,617 --> 00:00:15,469
Six steps of pandas, and every one of those problems is gone.

5
00:00:16,169 --> 00:00:21,280
Today you'll write that cleaning script yourself, step by step, even if you're new to Python.

6
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:30,130
You'll learn how to find the problems first, fix each one with a single line, and avoid the three mistakes that make cleaning code silently do nothing, or crash.

7
00:00:30,829 --> 00:00:31,741
Here's the plan.

8
00:00:31,741 --> 00:00:33,394
Load the file and look at it.

9
00:00:33,394 --> 00:00:36,129
Run a quick health check, to count the problems.

10
00:00:36,129 --> 00:00:37,896
Then fix them in a smart order.

11
00:00:37,896 --> 00:00:41,885
Text first, then duplicates, then dates and numbers, then empty cells.

12
00:00:41,885 --> 00:00:44,449
And finally, save a clean copy, and check it.

13
00:00:45,149 --> 00:00:45,774
Why bother?

14
00:00:45,774 --> 00:00:49,411
Because messy data gives you wrong answers, without any warning.

15
00:00:49,411 --> 00:00:55,492
In this file, Boston, boston, and Boston with a space at the end, are three different cities to a computer.

16
00:00:55,492 --> 00:00:58,219
Prices with a dollar sign aren't numbers at all.

17
00:00:58,219 --> 00:01:02,084
At the end of this video, I'll show you how far off the totals were.

18
00:01:02,084 --> 00:01:03,675
It's not a small difference.

19
00:01:04,375 --> 00:01:07,668
You need Python, free from python dot org, and pandas.

20
00:01:07,668 --> 00:01:10,413
Open a terminal, and type pip install pandas.

21
00:01:10,413 --> 00:01:12,426
Then check it with this one line.

22
00:01:12,426 --> 00:01:14,988
If you see a version number, you're ready.

23
00:01:14,988 --> 00:01:17,183
Mine says three point one point one.

24
00:01:17,884 --> 00:01:20,426
Here's our practice file, orders dot c s v.

25
00:01:20,426 --> 00:01:25,748
A C S V is just a table saved as plain text, one row per line, commas between the columns.

26
00:01:25,748 --> 00:01:28,291
Excel and Google Sheets can both export it.

27
00:01:28,291 --> 00:01:29,947
Look at the first few lines.

28
00:01:29,947 --> 00:01:31,603
You can already see trouble.

29
00:01:31,603 --> 00:01:38,522
Denver in capitals, a date with slashes, extra spaces after a name, and some prices with a dollar sign, some without.

30
00:01:39,222 --> 00:01:39,822
Step one.

31
00:01:39,822 --> 00:01:43,422
Create a file called clean dot p y, next to the C S V.

32
00:01:43,422 --> 00:01:44,823
Import pandas as p d.

33
00:01:44,823 --> 00:01:47,223
That's the short name everyone uses.

34
00:01:47,223 --> 00:01:51,891
Read c s v loads the file into a data frame, pandas' name for a table.

35
00:01:51,891 --> 00:01:55,891
Print the first six rows with head, and the size with shape.

36
00:01:56,592 --> 00:01:57,009
Run it.

37
00:01:57,009 --> 00:02:01,004
Pandas hides the middle columns with three dots, so the table fits.

38
00:02:01,004 --> 00:02:04,105
And the shape says one thousand rows, eight columns.

39
00:02:04,105 --> 00:02:08,100
Notice the quantity column shows three point zero instead of three.

40
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:08,935
That's a clue.

41
00:02:08,935 --> 00:02:13,944
Some quantities are missing, and pandas uses decimals when a column has empty cells.

42
00:02:14,644 --> 00:02:16,211
Step two, the health check.

43
00:02:16,211 --> 00:02:20,913
Before fixing anything, count the problems, so you know what you're dealing with.

44
00:02:20,913 --> 00:02:25,033
Duplicated dot sum counts rows that are exact copies of an earlier row.

45
00:02:25,033 --> 00:02:27,877
N unique counts the different values in a column.

46
00:02:27,877 --> 00:02:31,244
And is n a dot sum counts the empty cells in every column.

47
00:02:31,244 --> 00:02:32,637
N a means not available.

48
00:02:33,337 --> 00:02:34,486
Here's the report.

49
00:02:34,486 --> 00:02:36,210
Twenty nine duplicate rows.

50
00:02:36,210 --> 00:02:39,849
Fifty different cities, in a shop that only sells in six.

51
00:02:39,849 --> 00:02:45,595
Twenty customers with no name, forty two orders with no city, and thirty with no quantity.

52
00:02:45,595 --> 00:02:47,191
Now we have a to do list.

53
00:02:47,891 --> 00:02:49,099
Step three, the text.

54
00:02:49,099 --> 00:02:54,447
For the customer and city columns, dot s t r gives you text tools for a whole column at once.

55
00:02:54,447 --> 00:02:56,863
Strip removes spaces at the start and end.

56
00:02:56,863 --> 00:02:59,853
Title makes the first letter of each word a capital.

57
00:02:59,853 --> 00:03:02,959
For status, we use lower, so everything is lower case.

58
00:03:02,959 --> 00:03:04,512
And notice the equals sign.

59
00:03:04,512 --> 00:03:07,100
We save the result back into the same column.

60
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:10,761
Here's what that does, on real rows from the file.

61
00:03:10,761 --> 00:03:13,367
The dots are the extra spaces, made visible.

62
00:03:13,367 --> 00:03:16,150
Watch them disappear, and the capitals line up.

63
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:22,251
Across the whole file, that's one thousand three hundred and three cells fixed, by three lines of code.

64
00:03:22,951 --> 00:03:25,851
Mistake number one, and almost everyone makes it.

65
00:03:25,851 --> 00:03:26,917
Look at line four.

66
00:03:26,917 --> 00:03:30,883
It strips and fixes the city, but doesn't save the result anywhere.

67
00:03:30,883 --> 00:03:33,725
Pandas makes a cleaned copy, and throws it away.

68
00:03:33,725 --> 00:03:37,040
Then on line seven, the same thing with the equals sign.

69
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:38,156
Run it.

70
00:03:38,156 --> 00:03:40,002
The first count is still fifty.

71
00:03:40,002 --> 00:03:42,979
Nothing changed, and there's no error to warn you.

72
00:03:42,979 --> 00:03:45,837
After the line with the equals sign, six cities.

73
00:03:45,837 --> 00:03:48,934
Chicago, Austin, Miami, Denver, Boston, and Seattle.

74
00:03:48,934 --> 00:03:52,447
If a fix seems to do nothing, check that you saved it back.

75
00:03:53,147 --> 00:03:53,824
Now, typos.

76
00:03:53,824 --> 00:03:56,410
The best way to find them is value counts.

77
00:03:56,410 --> 00:04:00,659
It lists every different value in a column, and how often it appears.

78
00:04:00,659 --> 00:04:03,800
After making status lower case, we get four values.

79
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,002
Shipped, pending, cancelled, and shiped, with one p.

80
00:04:07,002 --> 00:04:08,110
Forty eight times.

81
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:11,312
The fix is replace, with a small dictionary.

82
00:04:11,312 --> 00:04:14,667
The wrong spelling on the left, the right one on the right.

83
00:04:14,667 --> 00:04:16,828
You can add as many pairs as you need.

84
00:04:16,828 --> 00:04:18,647
And here it is on the real rows.

85
00:04:18,647 --> 00:04:20,296
Every shiped becomes shipped.

86
00:04:20,996 --> 00:04:22,388
Step five, duplicates.

87
00:04:22,388 --> 00:04:22,958
One line.

88
00:04:22,958 --> 00:04:25,046
Df equals df dot drop duplicates.

89
00:04:25,046 --> 00:04:28,653
It keeps the first copy of every row, and drops the rest.

90
00:04:28,653 --> 00:04:31,247
Watch rows twenty two and one fifty nine.

91
00:04:31,247 --> 00:04:35,993
Before the text fix, one said Zoe Hayes in capitals, with lowercase boston.

92
00:04:35,993 --> 00:04:38,904
Now they're identical, so the second one goes.

93
00:04:39,603 --> 00:04:41,372
And that's mistake number two.

94
00:04:41,372 --> 00:04:43,199
The order of the steps matters.

95
00:04:43,199 --> 00:04:46,854
If you remove duplicates first, pandas only finds twenty nine.

96
00:04:46,854 --> 00:04:49,153
Fix the text first, and it finds sixty.

97
00:04:49,153 --> 00:04:52,985
Thirty one copies were hiding behind a capital letter or a space.

98
00:04:52,985 --> 00:04:55,932
So, always clean the text, then remove duplicates.

99
00:04:56,632 --> 00:04:57,589
Step six, dates.

100
00:04:57,589 --> 00:05:00,282
Right now they're just text, in three styles.

101
00:05:00,282 --> 00:05:05,008
Year month day, month slash day slash year, and the month written out in words.

102
00:05:05,008 --> 00:05:08,418
Try the obvious fix, p d dot to datetime, and it crashes.

103
00:05:08,418 --> 00:05:13,324
Pandas guesses the format from the first row, and the second row doesn't match it.

104
00:05:14,023 --> 00:05:15,891
The error even tells you the fix.

105
00:05:15,891 --> 00:05:19,512
Pass format equals mixed, and pandas reads each date on its own.

106
00:05:19,512 --> 00:05:24,886
Now every date is a real date, written the same way, and you can sort by it, or group by month.

107
00:05:24,886 --> 00:05:25,565
One warning.

108
00:05:25,565 --> 00:05:29,582
If your dates are day first, like in Europe, add day first equals true.

109
00:05:29,582 --> 00:05:31,846
Otherwise March fifth becomes May third.

110
00:05:32,545 --> 00:05:33,784
Mistake number three.

111
00:05:33,784 --> 00:05:35,141
Numbers stored as text.

112
00:05:35,141 --> 00:05:38,268
You'd think to numeric turns the price into a number.

113
00:05:38,268 --> 00:05:40,451
But it crashes on the very first row.

114
00:05:40,451 --> 00:05:43,401
Unable to parse string, dollar thirty four ninety.

115
00:05:43,401 --> 00:05:47,530
A dollar sign and a comma aren't part of a number, so pandas gives up.

116
00:05:48,230 --> 00:05:49,706
So clean the text first.

117
00:05:49,706 --> 00:05:53,150
Str dot replace removes the dollar sign, then the comma.

118
00:05:53,150 --> 00:05:54,503
Then to numeric works.

119
00:05:54,503 --> 00:05:57,885
All nine hundred and forty prices are real numbers now.

120
00:05:57,885 --> 00:06:01,575
Pandas writes thirty four point nine, without the last zero.

121
00:06:01,575 --> 00:06:02,435
That's normal.

122
00:06:02,435 --> 00:06:03,727
It's the same number.

123
00:06:04,427 --> 00:06:05,906
Here it is on the rows.

124
00:06:05,906 --> 00:06:10,217
Dollar signs and commas gone, and every price lines up as a number.

125
00:06:10,917 --> 00:06:12,223
Last, the empty cells.

126
00:06:12,223 --> 00:06:14,062
There's no single right answer.

127
00:06:14,062 --> 00:06:15,368
You decide per column.

128
00:06:15,368 --> 00:06:21,541
An order with no customer, or no quantity, can't be trusted, so drop n a with subset removes those rows.

129
00:06:21,541 --> 00:06:25,043
A missing city is less serious, so fill n a writes Unknown.

130
00:06:25,043 --> 00:06:30,147
And now that quantity has no gaps, as type int turns three point zero back into three.

131
00:06:30,847 --> 00:06:31,223
Watch.

132
00:06:31,223 --> 00:06:33,793
Rows nineteen and ninety two are dropped.

133
00:06:33,793 --> 00:06:37,616
Row fifty four keeps its order, and gets Unknown as the city.

134
00:06:37,616 --> 00:06:41,565
That's forty eight rows removed, and thirty four cities filled.

135
00:06:42,265 --> 00:06:43,647
Here's the whole script.

136
00:06:43,647 --> 00:06:48,368
Load, fix the text, fix the typo, drop duplicates, dates, prices, and empty cells.

137
00:06:48,368 --> 00:06:52,283
At the end, print the new shape, and the number of empty cells left.

138
00:06:52,283 --> 00:06:55,392
And to c s v saves a new file, orders clean dot c s v.

139
00:06:55,392 --> 00:06:57,061
Never overwrite the original.

140
00:06:57,061 --> 00:07:00,919
If something goes wrong, you want to start again from the raw data.

141
00:07:01,619 --> 00:07:02,074
Run it.

142
00:07:02,074 --> 00:07:03,762
Before, one thousand rows.

143
00:07:03,762 --> 00:07:06,101
After, eight hundred and ninety two.

144
00:07:06,101 --> 00:07:07,595
Empty cells left, zero.

145
00:07:07,595 --> 00:07:07,985
Saved.

146
00:07:08,685 --> 00:07:10,069
Always check the result.

147
00:07:10,069 --> 00:07:14,567
Read the clean file back, with parse dates, so the date column loads as dates.

148
00:07:14,567 --> 00:07:15,893
Look at the first rows.

149
00:07:15,893 --> 00:07:16,182
Neat.

150
00:07:16,182 --> 00:07:18,661
And d types shows the type of every column.

151
00:07:18,661 --> 00:07:23,505
Date is a date time, quantity is an integer, and price is a float, a decimal number.

152
00:07:23,505 --> 00:07:25,120
That's exactly what we want.

153
00:07:25,820 --> 00:07:26,310
Pro tip.

154
00:07:26,310 --> 00:07:28,206
Here's why all of this matters.

155
00:07:28,206 --> 00:07:32,979
This script computes revenue by city, on the messy file, and on the clean one.

156
00:07:32,979 --> 00:07:38,607
On the messy file, pandas can't read the prices with dollar signs, so it quietly skips them.

157
00:07:39,307 --> 00:07:44,036
The messy file says fifty cities, and five hundred and fifty six thousand dollars.

158
00:07:44,036 --> 00:07:49,572
The clean file says seven cities, counting Unknown, and seven hundred and eighty three thousand.

159
00:07:49,572 --> 00:07:54,761
The messy report was off by more than two hundred thousand dollars, and nothing warned us.

160
00:07:55,461 --> 00:07:56,304
And the speed?

161
00:07:56,304 --> 00:07:59,917
I timed the full cleaning script on these one thousand rows.

162
00:07:59,917 --> 00:08:01,242
A quarter of a second.

163
00:08:01,242 --> 00:08:05,155
And next month, when a new export arrives, you just run it again.

164
00:08:05,855 --> 00:08:07,363
Here's your cheat sheet.

165
00:08:07,363 --> 00:08:09,311
Strip and title for messy text.

166
00:08:09,311 --> 00:08:12,578
Value counts to find typos, and replace to fix them.

167
00:08:12,578 --> 00:08:14,588
Drop duplicates, after the text.

168
00:08:14,588 --> 00:08:16,473
To datetime with format mixed.

169
00:08:16,473 --> 00:08:18,798
Replace, then to numeric, for prices.

170
00:08:18,798 --> 00:08:21,373
And drop n a or fill n a for empty cells.

171
00:08:22,073 --> 00:08:22,803
Quick recap.

172
00:08:22,803 --> 00:08:24,932
Look first, and run a health check.

173
00:08:24,932 --> 00:08:28,095
Fix the text, and save it back with the equals sign.

174
00:08:28,095 --> 00:08:30,102
Remove duplicates after the text.

175
00:08:30,102 --> 00:08:33,143
Turn dates and prices into real dates and numbers.

176
00:08:33,143 --> 00:08:35,272
Decide what to do with empty cells.

177
00:08:35,272 --> 00:08:36,610
And save a clean copy.

178
00:08:37,310 --> 00:08:46,242
Next on Shortcut Lab, we take this clean table, and turn it into an automatic Excel report, with totals by city and by month, in one script.

179
00:08:46,242 --> 00:08:48,284
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
